\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Tennis Grand Slams are contested across three distinct structural profiles: grass at Wimbledon, clay at the French Open, and hardcourts at the Australian and US Opens. The surfaces may fundamentally alter game strategies and communication dynamics.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{melitztoubal2014}
\citation{psw2024}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2}Data}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.1}Empirical Setting and Sample}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Observation Breakdown and Descriptive Statistics\relax }}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:t1}{{1}{7}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {2.2}Measuring Cultural Similarity [rewrite!]}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Shares of the Three Cultural Similarity Measures, and Unique Teams by Tournament\relax }}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t2}{{2}{8}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Theory and Hypotheses}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Hypothesis 1 (H1): In organic, bilaterally self-sorted teams operating under tight real-time constraints, cultural and linguistic similarity is positively associated with team performance when strategic communication windows are available.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Hypothesis 2 (H2): Under an acute time shock that structurally eliminates strategic communication windows, the positive association between cultural similarity and team performance disappears.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.1}Cultural Similarity and Team Performance}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}Tiebreaks as a Boundary Condition}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Tiebreaks impose a different temporal and competitive environment in which the opportunity for any coordination advantage to accumulate is substantially reduced.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}The Moderating Pathways of Professional Experience}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In our sample, no genuine 10-point match tiebreak is ever used to decide the last set at any of the four Grand Slams. Wimbledon is the only tournament with a non-standard deciding-set format: in 15 of our sample's matches (7 in 2018, 3 in 2019, 5 in 2021), the last set was instead resolved through extended advantage-set play with no breaker at all, with final scores such as 16--14 and 22--20 -- consistent with Wimbledon's pre-2019 rule of no tiebreak in the deciding set and, for the 2019 and 2021 cases, with its subsequent 12-12-breaker rule simply never being triggered because the set ended before reaching 12-12. At the Australian Open, Roland Garros, and US Open -- and in the remaining 79 Wimbledon matches with a completed deciding set -- the last set is instead resolved by the same standard 6--6 seven-point breaker used in every other set.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{We further refine this framework by exploring how the total pool and internal distribution of team expertise alter the utilization of this shared communication channel. We evaluate two distinct human capital pathways through which experience modulates the homophily premium. First, a shared cultural background may function as a conduit that helps highly experienced teammates rapidly integrate their deep, individual repositories of task-specific knowledge into a unified strategy during tight strategic windows. If cultural similarity complements accumulated professional human capital, the homophily premium should strengthen with the overall maturity of the unit (team average experience). Second, cultural similarity may act as a vehicle for internal knowledge transfer, facilitating real-time instruction and coaching between senior and junior peers, implying that the premium should increase with the within-team experience gap.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Empirical Analysis}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.1}Match Performance}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{matchoutcome}{{1}{11}}
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The ATP does not publish a persistent, tournament-by-tournament doubles ranking at the pair level: the doubles rankings we scrape are strictly individual, one entry per player, with no team or pairing identifier. The only ATP ranking computed at the pair level is the season-only Doubles Race, used exclusively to seed qualification for the year-end ATP Finals; it resets every calendar year and is defined only for pairs that have accumulated enough joint results together that season, so it does not exist for a large share of our sample -- doubles partnerships change frequently across tournaments, and many Grand Slam pairings are new or infrequent. Each player's own individual ranking, by contrast, is continuously available regardless of current partner, so we use the two teammates' average individual ranking as the team-quality measure.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t3}{{3}{12}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Match Win — Average Marginal Effects (AME) from Logit \relax }}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Alternative specification: randomly assign one team per match as the focal team to be kept in the regression analysis (N=1,864). Coefficients are of similar magnitude and slighter weaker significance.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}Robustness check across Playing Surfaces}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Tennis Grand Slams are contested across three distinct structural profiles: grass at Wimbledon, clay at the French Open, and hardcourts at the Australian and US Opens. These surfaces fundamentally alter ball dynamics, where clay generates high friction that slows the ball and increases bounce height, grass minimizes friction to produce low, rapid trajectories, and hardcourts offer a standardized, intermediate speed and bounce. Consequently, these physical variances dictate distinct communication styles. On fast surface like grass, the rapid pace compresses decision-making windows, forcing partners to rely on pre-packaged tactical scripts and implicit, non-verbal cues because there is no time for mid-rally adjustments. Conversely, on slow surface like clay, longer rallies allow for active, asynchronous verbal feedback during the point, as well as more frequent emotional regulation and strategic recalibration between points to sustain high cognitive load.}}{13}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t5}{{4}{14}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Stability across playing surfaces \relax }}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.3}Testing Tiebreaks as a Boundary Condition}{14}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t4}{{5}{16}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Tiebreak Win — Average Marginal Effects (AME) from Logit \relax }}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{If culturally similar teams were simply stronger for some persistent reason, we would have some reason to expect that advantage to remain visible in tiebreaks.}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}The Moderating Pathways of Professional Experience}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.4.1}Average Professional Experience}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Notice that this is not the years the two players have been working together in the same team, but the average individual professional experience of the two players.}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t6}{{6}{18}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Team Average Experience \relax }}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In tennis, partnership can change relatively often. Players may team up with different partners in different tournaments. Calculate how many teams and the share in total have repeated partnership during the sample period and how many unique team appearance.}}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {4.4.2}Experience Gaps within the Team}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The experience-gap analysis asks whether cultural similarity is particularly valuable when teammates differ in professional experience, conditional on the team's overall competitive strength and average professional experience. That is, among teams with comparable overall strength and comparable average professional experience, does cultural similarity become particularly valuable when the two teammates differ more in professional experience?}}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t6b}{{7}{20}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Within-Team Experience Gap \relax }}{20}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}Team Formation and Cultural Sorting}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Partnership formation in professional doubles tennis is inherently multidimensional. Players may sort according to ability, experience, playing style, professional relationships, or commercial connections, among other considerations. We focus here on one dimension of this broader matching process: cultural similarity.}}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.1}Cultural Sorting in Partnership Formation}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:sorting}{{5.1}{22}}
\newlabel{tab:t8}{{8}{23}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Cultural Sorting in Partnership Formation: Actual vs.\ Random-Matching Benchmark \relax }}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.2}Player Ranking and Partner Selection}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Because the cultural composition of the player pool differs across tournament-years, a player's probability of having a culturally similar partner may vary mechanically with the set of players entering the event. We therefore extend the specification to also account for the cultural composition of the tournament field: for each focal player, the share of every \emph  {other} player in that tournament-year's full field (every entrant, not only the rank-complete partnerships used above) who shares the focal player's nationality -- and, analogously, the mean pairwise value of \textit  {same\_language}/\textit  {ling\_prox} against every other player in the field -- matched to the corresponding outcome.}}{24}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{We estimate three specifications: (1) own ranking alone (as above); (2) the outcome-matched field-composition variable alone; and (3) own ranking plus field composition. All three specifications exclude the 3 ego-rows whose own nationality is represented by exactly one player in the ego-row sample, so that a single lone-nationality observation cannot by itself drive any coefficient; this leaves $N=4{,}175$ (from 4,178) throughout.}}{24}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:t9}{{9}{25}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Own Ranking, Tournament-Field Composition, and Partner-Culture Similarity \relax }}{25}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Field composition alone (Spec 2) is also a strong, highly significant predictor of all three outcomes, as expected -- a player drawn from a tournament field with more compatriots or linguistically similar entrants is mechanically more likely to end up with a culturally similar partner. Adding both own ranking and field composition together (Spec 3) shrinks the own-ranking coefficients by roughly half relative to Spec 1 but leaves them positive and statistically significant for all three outcomes (p$<$0.05 throughout), while the composition coefficient stays close to its Spec 2 magnitude. Own ranking therefore predicts partner similarity \emph  {in addition to}, not merely \emph  {because of}, the cultural composition of the field a player happens to be drawn from.}}{25}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}Conclusion}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In a world filled with global freelance platforms, decentralized autonomous organizations (DAOs), academic networks, and highly autonomous consultancies, individuals act as their own human resource managers.}}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Attention: tennis doubles are formed by individual players, not by a manager or an organization. Different implications if were assigned by managers/organizations like football. The findings also have implications for organizations in which employees have discretion over their collaborators. In such settings, team composition may emerge partly through endogenous cultural sorting rather than solely through managerial assignment. If cultural similarity facilitates coordination, such sorting may reflect an economically meaningful feature of decentralized team formation. At the same time, our results do not imply that managers should systematically favor culturally similar teams: the evidence is observational, and culturally homogeneous partnerships may also reflect selection into partnerships on unobserved characteristics.}}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{While prior literature often evaluates team diversity through the lens of centralized, top-down managerial assignment, our findings offer vital insights for the expanding landscape of decentralized, self-organized collaborations. For the autonomous professional navigating global knowledge markets, we show that cultural homophily is not merely a psychological comfort, but a rational coordination subsidy that enhances performance under real-time strategic constraints. However, we identify a sharp boundary condition for individual strategy: the value of this cultural conduit is strictly dependent on the availability of strategic communication windows and the total pool of accumulated human capital. When tasks transition into hyper-velocity execution shocks governed by universal, protocol-driven signaling, the homophily premium evaporates. For organizational designers, this reveals that team performance can be dynamically managed not by forcing composition, but by structurally altering the temporal architecture of the task environment.}}{27}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The performance relevance of cultural similarity depends on whether the structure of the task allows communication and coordination advantages to accumulate.}}{28}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Cultural similarity may be more valuable when teams perform tasks requiring repeated interdependent coordination, communication, and adaptation. The value may be less visible when tasks are sufficiently short, tightly constrained, or dominated by small differences in performance. Managers should think about cultural composition jointly with task structure, rather than treating cultural similarity as inherently good or bad. Our evidence suggests that its performance relevance depends on whether team members have sufficient opportunity to communicate, adjust, and coordinate their actions over the course of a task.}}{28}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{While our empirical setting offers unique advantages in terms of observable effort, organic team formation, and clean performance metrics, several limitations remain that present fruitful avenues for future research. First, we emphasize that our empirical strategy identifies robust conditional associations rather than definitive causal effects. Although our models include comprehensive controls for individual and opponent capabilities, team experience, and tournament-by-year fixed effects—and our subsequent analysis rules out the alternative explanation that higher-ability actors systematically sort into homogeneous clusters—we cannot entirely eliminate the presence of unobservable, time-varying confounders. Factors such as unmeasured personal compatibility, real-time physical fatigue, or unobserved shared training histories could simultaneously influence both the decision to collaborate and the probability of match success. Future research utilizing true exogenous assignment of partners within high-velocity, real-time execution environments would be required to firmly establish a causal interpretation of the coordination channels we document. Second, while our findings demonstrate that the positive association between cultural similarity and performance completely disappears under the acute time shocks of tiebreaks, our data structure does not allow us to directly record the exact verbal or non-verbal content of the communication occurring during the 90-second changeovers versus between points. We infer that this null finding is driven by a structural shift from explicit strategic troubleshooting to standardized, protocol-driven tactical signaling. Future organizational studies could build upon our framework by capturing granular audio or visual tracking of self-organized teams in action, explicitly measuring how the linguistic complexity of real-time pivots changes as communication windows shrink. Finally, our sample is exclusively restricted to professional men's teams operating in a hyper-competitive sports environment. Examining whether these conditional associations and temporal boundaries hold in other decentralized, self-organized knowledge settings—such as cross-border high-frequency trading duos or remote open-source software crisis units—would greatly enhance the generalizability of our findings.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{melitztoubal2014}
